      "final_message": "Implemented coordinated two-die CTS in [three_d_cts.py](/workspace/open3dbench/backend/three_d_cts.py:595).\n\nKey changes:\n\n- Measures full passive HBT plus receiving-tree delay, then feeds it back as a real TritonCTS insertion-delay model.\n- Rebuilds the source-side tree once with clock-net repair before native latency balancing.\n- Compares baseline and coordinated trees using authoritative passive, per-clock/active-edge skew.\n- Rejects changed sink/domain ownership and falls back unless skew improves beyond numerical tolerance.\n- Enforces a conservative 2\u00d7 source-tree buffer guard; setup/hold remains diagnostic.\n- Preserves separate seed/coordinated models and publishes detailed selection evidence in `metrics.json`.\n\nAdded targeted tests in [test_cross_die_cts.py](/workspace/engineering/tests/test_cross_die_cts.py:38), covering normal coordination, little-offset fallback, setup/hold independence, auxiliary-clock ownership, resource limits, and insertion-delay construction.\n\nEvidence:\n\n- 60 targeted Python tests passed; one optional sealed-input test skipped.\n- Real OpenROAD CTS smoke test inserted three latency-balancing buffers with zero unconnected buffers.\n- No full-case EDA search or benchmark evaluation was run.\n- No native C++ files changed, so no candidate-image compilation is required.\n\nExpected\u2014not measured\u2014benefit is reduced cross-die skew when receiving-tree latency dominates. Final QoR, electrical nonregression, legality, and exact area are still determined by the unchanged harness legalization and independent placement-RC evaluator. The method uses one bounded source-tree rebuild and remains placement-estimated rather than routed signoff timing.",
